Name     AUDIO_SLOT;
PartNo   00;
Date     2026-10-16;
Revision 03;
Designer Justin;
Company  ;
Assembly ;
Location ;
Device   g22v10;


/* ============================================================
   BLANKING READS AND ROW ADDRESS   (ATF22V10C, DIP-24 / PLCC-28)
   ============================================================

   Picks the 8-clock groups on each scan line where the video
   circuit reads a byte in horizontal blanking: the line's mode
   byte (see VIDEO.md) and the audio sample (see SOUND.md).  Since
   revision 03 it also remaps four row address bits for the line
   repeat modes.

   The reads themselves are made by the Sync PLD: BLANK_RD goes to
   Sync pin 13, and Sync raises PIXEL_ENABLE in clock 1 of each
   group it marks (the same clock as a pixel read).  The read
   address is the video address, H9:H3 on A15:A9 and the row
   address (R8:R0 below) on A8:A0.  Each read lands in the pixel
   buffer.  Video reads always come from the RAM.

   REVISION 03 (2026-10-16)
      The I/O window shrank to 5000h-53FFh, so the blanking reads
      moved up into 5400h-5FFFh and 4000h-4FFFh is free for
      programs.  Before: mode byte in group 37 (4A20h + row), audio
      in groups 38/39 (4DF3h-4FFFh), DAC clocked by HSYNC.

   MODE BYTE
      Group 45 on every line, H9:H3 = 0101101, so the mode byte of
      line V is at 5A00h + V8:V0.  Visible rows 0-479 are lines
      544-1023, V8:V0 = 32-511, so row r's mode byte is at
      5A20h + r (5A20h-5BFFh).

      /MODE_LD (pin 22) is low for the whole of group 45.  It is the
      /E of the line code register (F377), whose D inputs come from
      the pixel buffer outputs.  The pixel buffer loads the mode byte
      at the end of clock 1, and the line code register loads it
      again on each of clocks 2-7, so it holds the mode byte when
      /MODE_LD goes high at group 46.  It keeps it for the rest of the
      line, through the audio read (groups 46/47) and all of the
      active video (groups 48-127).

   AUDIO SAMPLE, TWO GROUPS
      The V counter runs 499-1023, 525 lines, but only V8:V0 reach
      the address.  Lines 499-511 and 1011-1023 have the same V8:V0,
      so a read in one fixed group would repeat 13 samples every
      frame (a 60 Hz buzz).  Reading in group 46 when V9 = 0 and in
      group 47 when V9 = 1 puts V9 on A9 (H3), so every line reads
      its own byte:

          lines      V9  group  H9:H3     address
          499-511    0   46     0101110   5C00h + 499..511 = 5DF3h-5DFFh
          512-1023   1   47     0101111   5E00h + 0..511   = 5E00h-5FFFh

      One frame plays 5DF3h-5FFFh in order: 525 bytes, one per line.

   DAC CLOCK
      The audio read now comes after HSYNC ends (group 42), so the
      DAC register (74HCT574) can't be clocked by HSYNC any more.  It
      is clocked by H_VIDEO_ACTIVE (H counter PLD pin 22) instead,
      which is registered and rises at the start of group 48.  The
      pixel buffer holds the sample until the first pixel read of
      group 48 loads it (end of clock 1), so the edge (H counter tCO
      5.5 ns into group 48) copies the sample.  On lines 499-511 the
      sample was read in group 46, so it waits one more group; every
      sample still lasts one line.  A registered pin can't glitch,
      which a clock decoded here from H9:H3 could at group changes.

   ORDER IN THE LINE
      45 mode byte, 46/47 audio sample, start of 48 the DAC register
      copies the sample (H_VIDEO_ACTIVE rises), 48 the first pixel.  The mode read must come
      before the audio read, or it would overwrite the sample in the
      pixel buffer before the DAC copies it.  Groups 40-41 are the
      I/O window (5000h-53FFh), where no memory answers.

   UNIQUE GROUP
      The H counter runs 28-127.  !H9 & H8 & !H7 & H6 is groups
      40-47 only.  H5 & H4 narrows it to 46 and 47, and H3 = V9
      picks one.  H5 & !H4 & H3 is group 45.

   V9 changes at the end of the last clock of a line (H_TC), so
   during groups 45-47 it is the new line's V9.

   ROW ADDRESS (line repeat, revision 03)
      Bits 3:2 of the line code (LC3:LC2, the line code register's
      Q3 and Q2) pick how many lines show each row of video memory:
      00 every line (1x), 01 two lines (2x), 10 or 11 four lines (4x).
      The address mux's video inputs for A0, A1, A7 and A8 come from
      R0, R1, R7 and R8 here instead of straight from the V counter
      (A2-A6 are still V2-V6):

          address   1x   2x   4x
          A0        V0   V8   V7
          A1        V1   V1   V8
          A7        V7   V7   1
          A8        V8   1    1

      In 2x the row address no longer depends on V0, so two lines
      read the same byte; in 4x it doesn't depend on V1:V0.  The
      high bits are held at 1, so 2x rows use the upper half of each
      byte column (offsets 256-511) and 4x rows the upper quarter
      (384-511), and the rest of the column is free.  The bits that
      would vary go to the bottom (a rotate), so every 2x pair and
      every 4x group still has its own byte.  1x rows 0-223 (offsets
      32-255) never share a byte with a 2x or 4x row.

      RAW is groups 32-47 (!H9 & !H7), where the blanking reads use
      the plain V8:V0, so the mode byte and the audio sample are
      read from one byte per line whatever the line code says.  The
      active groups 48-127 all have H9 or H7 set.  (Groups 28-31
      also have H7 set but make no reads.)  The line code changes in
      group 45, inside RAW, so it only affects the active reads.

      Timing: RAW switches at the start of group 48, and R0-R8 settle
      13 ns later (H counter tCO 5.5 ns + 7.5 ns); the first pixel
      read is in clock 1.  V changes once per line and LC3:LC2 in
      group 45, both long before.

   PIN BUDGET
      Inputs (14): H9-H3, V9, V0, V1, V7, V8, LC2, LC3.
      Outputs (6): BLANK_RD, /MODE_LD, R0, R1, R7, R8.
      Unused: pins 16, 21.
      BLANK_RD is combinational, 3 of 8 product terms on pin 23.
      /MODE_LD is combinational, 1 of 10 product terms on pin 22.
      R0, R1, R7, R8 expand to at most 6 product terms (RAW and !RAW
      are each one or two terms); pins 17-20 have 14-16.
   ============================================================ */


/* ---------------- INPUTS ---------------- */

PIN 1  = V0;          /* V counter PLD pin 14 (Q0) */
PIN 2  = H9;          /* H counter PLD pin 20 */
PIN 3  = H8;          /* H counter PLD pin 19 */
PIN 4  = H7;          /* H counter PLD pin 18 */
PIN 5  = H6;          /* H counter PLD pin 17 */
PIN 6  = H5;          /* H counter PLD pin 16 */
PIN 7  = H4;          /* H counter PLD pin 15 */
PIN 8  = H3;          /* H counter PLD pin 14 */
PIN 9  = V9;          /* V counter PLD pin 19 (Q9) */
PIN 10 = V1;          /* V counter PLD pin 23 (Q1) */
PIN 11 = V7;          /* V counter PLD pin 17 (Q7) */
PIN 13 = V8;          /* V counter PLD pin 18 (Q8) */
PIN 14 = LC2;         /* line code register Q2 (I/O pin used as input) */
PIN 15 = LC3;         /* line code register Q3 (I/O pin used as input) */


/* ---------------- OUTPUTS ---------------- */

PIN 17 = R0;          /* to address mux bits 3:0, C0 input (video A0) */
PIN 18 = R1;          /* to address mux bits 3:0, C1 input (video A1) */
PIN 19 = R7;          /* to address mux bits 7:4, C3 input (video A7) */
PIN 20 = R8;          /* to address mux bits 11:8, C0 input (video A8) */
PIN 22 = !MODE_LD;    /* to line code register (F377) /E, low for the whole mode group */
PIN 23 = BLANK_RD;    /* to Sync PLD pin 13, high for the whole audio or mode group */


/* ---------------- EQUATIONS ---------------- */

BLANK_RD = !H9 &  H8 & !H7 &  H6 &  H5 &  H4 &  H3 &  V9     /* group 47, lines 512-1023 */
         # !H9 &  H8 & !H7 &  H6 &  H5 &  H4 & !H3 & !V9     /* group 46, lines 499-511  */
         # !H9 &  H8 & !H7 &  H6 &  H5 & !H4 &  H3;          /* group 45, every line     */

MODE_LD  = !H9 &  H8 & !H7 &  H6 &  H5 & !H4 &  H3;          /* group 45, every line     */

RAW      = !H9 & !H7;                                        /* groups 32-47: blanking reads */

R0 = V0 & RAW
   # V0 & !LC3 & !LC2
   # V8 & !RAW & !LC3 &  LC2                                 /* 2x */
   # V7 & !RAW &  LC3;                                       /* 4x */

R1 = V1 & RAW
   # V1 & !LC3
   # V8 & !RAW &  LC3;                                       /* 4x */

R7 = V7 & RAW
   # V7 & !LC3
   # !RAW & LC3;                                             /* 4x: 1 */

R8 = V8 & RAW
   # V8 & !LC3 & !LC2
   # !RAW & LC3                                              /* 4x: 1 */
   # !RAW & LC2;                                             /* 2x: 1 */
